{"uncertainty": 0.2, "outcome": "rtl_bug", "artifacts": [{"kind": "root_cause_analysis", "title": "Root cause of the failing uart_tx regression test: the data holding register reloads while a frame is in flight", "summary": "Requirement [requirement:req-d10c4df532]. Inputs: the approved RTL [artifact:prj-d4a591eb4b.design-under-investigation.a1], reviewed per [evidence:prj-d4a591eb4b.design-under-investigation.e1]. Triage evidence: [evidence:prj-d4a591eb4b.triage.e1] ([run:run-0001]) classified the log as unknown_failure at 30% confidence, with 'Likely RTL bug' as the top hypothesis. I treat that as one candidate only. [evidence:prj-d4a591eb4b.triage.e2] ([run:run-0002]) records the triage repro reaching $finish with 1 of 4 checks failed.\n\nDEFECT (from reading the source). The module header in [artifact:prj-d4a591eb4b.design-under-investigation.a1] states: 'A start pulse while a frame is in flight is ignored.' The always block contains an unconditional `if (start) hold <= data;` placed before the state case. It therefore updates `hold` in every state, including START, DATA and STOP. The IDLE branch already loads `hold` on start, so the outer statement is redundant in IDLE and wrong everywhere else.\n\nCAUSALITY. A start pulse during DATA overwrites `hold`. The later bits are driven from `tx <= hold[bit_idx + 3'd1]`, so the remaining data bits of the frame in flight come from the new byte. The frame on the wire becomes a mix of two bytes. A check of the serial bits against the originally requested byte fails at the first bit position where the two bytes differ.\n\nINCORRECTNESS. The header clause names the required behaviour: the pulse is ignored. Any correct implementation must latch `data` only when IDLE accepts the start.\n\nFIX DIRECTION, not applied here. Remove the outer `if (start) hold <= data;` and keep the load inside IDLE.\n\nRANKED HYPOTHESES, with the evidence that would refute each:\n(1) rtl_bug, mid-frame reload of hold. Most likely. It is refuted if the repro uart_tx_rca_repro.v gate run shows all checks passing on [artifact:prj-d4a591eb4b.design-under-investigation.a1]. It is also refuted if the frame without an injected start fails too, which would point elsewhere.\n(2) testbench_bug, a wrong expected value in the regression or triage checks. Ranked lower because the defect above is visible in the source independently of any check. My repro derives its expected bits from the header clause, not from RTL output. It is refuted as the sole cause if the new repro also fails only on the frame with a mid-frame start.\n(3) infrastructure. Ranked lowest. [evidence:prj-d4a591eb4b.triage.e2] shows a build that reached $finish and produced a counted verdict, which is not the signature of a tool or build fault. It would be supported if a clean rebuild changed the result with no source change; that experiment is not recorded.\n(4) spec_ambiguity. Rejected. The header clause explicitly fixes the behaviour for a start pulse while busy, so the expected value is not open to interpretation. It would be revived only if a governing specification outside this file contradicted the header; none is given to me.\n\nREPRO. The repro is uart_tx_rca_repro.v, a self-checking testbench of the specified behaviour. Frame 1 sends 0x5A with no disturbance and should pass on any correct RTL. Frame 2 sends 0xA5 and pulses start with 0x3C during data bit 1. With the defect, data bits 3, 4 and 7 come out as 0x3C's bits, so frame 2 should fail. Without the defect, frame 2 should pass. Whether it actually fails on the approved RTL is for the platform's simulator.run gate to establish; I report no such run."}], "files": [{"path": "uart_tx_rca_repro.v", "kind": "testbench", "title": "Self-checking repro: a start pulse during a frame must be ignored", "summary": "Repro for the defect diagnosed in [artifact:prj-d4a591eb4b.design-under-investigation.a1]. It instantiates uart_tx with CLKS_PER_BIT=4 and samples tx at mid-bit for 10 bit periods per frame.